{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-128186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the function $L(x),$ the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $L(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(1)=2.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=x{y}^{2},$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(1)=1\\cdot {2}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(1)$ and ${y}^{′}(1)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+4(x-1) \\\\  & =2+4x-4 \\\\  & =4x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ddd149e61113f0ba9be0dc932e525b10c73bcb40bf913ff8069c0906fb9584fc"} {"question" "q-128186" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128186" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4x-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the function $L(x),$ the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $L(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4x-2$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ found in part 1 to estimate the value of $y(\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Our tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =4x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{3}{2}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})-2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ddd149e61113f0ba9be0dc932e525b10c73bcb40bf913ff8069c0906fb9584fc"} {"question" "q-128187" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128187" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the function $L(x),$ the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $L(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4x-2$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ found in part 1 to estimate the value of $y(\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWe wish to improve the approximation to $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ found in step $2.$ To do this, we create a table and use Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$ The first row of our table is shown below. Find the missing values.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $a$ | $b$ | $c$ |\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $b=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, $a=2,$ $b=4,$ and $c=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ddd149e61113f0ba9be0dc932e525b10c73bcb40bf913ff8069c0906fb9584fc"} {"question" "q-128188" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128188" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128188" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128188" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128188" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128188" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the function $L(x),$ the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $L(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4x-2$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ found in part 1 to estimate the value of $y(\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nWe wish to improve the approximation to $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ found in step $2.$ To do this, we create a table and use Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$ The first row of our table is shown below. Find the missing values.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $a$ | $b$ | $c$ |\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $b=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nUse Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ using two steps of equal size.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We'll start with the table from part 3.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now proceed to approximate $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ using Euler's method.\n\n**Step 1.** We compute the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+1=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $3$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{2}=\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2}=\\frac{45}{4} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{45}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{45}{16} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+\\frac{45}{16}=\\frac{93}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{45}{4}$ | $\\frac{45}{16}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{93}{16}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈\\frac{93}{16}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ddd149e61113f0ba9be0dc932e525b10c73bcb40bf913ff8069c0906fb9584fc"} {"question" "q-128189" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128189" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{93}{16}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the function $L(x),$ the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $L(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4x-2$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ found in part 1 to estimate the value of $y(\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nWe wish to improve the approximation to $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ found in step $2.$ To do this, we create a table and use Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$ The first row of our table is shown below. Find the missing values.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $a$ | $b$ | $c$ |\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $b=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nUse Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ using two steps of equal size.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{93}{16}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{y(x)-2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that plugging in $x=1$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{y(x)-2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)} & =\\frac{2-2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{y(x)-2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{[y(x)-2]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{y}^{′}(x)}{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(1)}{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (y(2))^{2}}{-\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1\\cdot {2}^{2}}{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039183/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63a19903454870345cc479d4734f172538a39d1bb775ef7cec23c3e76790211"} {"question" "q-128190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :ques