c{3}{2}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nWe wish to improve the approximation to $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ found in step $2.$ To do this, we create a table and use Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$ The first row of our table is shown below. Find the missing values.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $a$ | $b$ | $c$ |\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                              